---
components: ["ShellBanner"]
description: Shell banners show an app-wide state, such as staging, impersonation, an incident, or being offline. They sit above the UI Shell header or at the top of the page content.
---

<script>
  import {
    NotificationActionButton,
    ShellBanner,
    Stack,
  } from "carbon-components-svelte";
</script>

## Basic

Place a `ShellBanner` with `placement="above-header"` next to the
[UI Shell](/components/UIShell) header. It is fixed to the top of the
viewport and moves the header, side navigation, header panels,
`Content` and the default [NotificationQueue](/components/NotificationQueue)
position down by its own height. Open the example in a new tab to try it.

<FileSource src="/framed/ShellBanner/ShellBannerAboveHeader" />

The banner publishes its height as the `--ccs-shell-offset` custom
property on `<html>`, and everything that is fixed to the top reads it.
Use it for anything of your own that is fixed under the header:

```css
.toolbar {
  position: fixed;
  top: calc(var(--ccs-shell-offset, 0px) + 3rem);
}
```

The server markup reserves 2.5rem before the page measures the banner,
so a one-line banner does not shift the layout when the page loads. A
banner whose text wraps moves the shell once, when the page mounts.

## Placement

Set `placement` to choose where the banner sits.

| Use case | Placement | Dismissible |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Staging or sandbox environment | `"above-header"` | No |
| Impersonation | `"above-header"` | No, it has an exit action |
| Offline or reconnecting | `"above-header"` | No |
| Incident or degraded service | `"content"` | Yes |
| Maintenance window | `"content"` | Yes |
| Announcement of a release | `"above-header"` | Yes, with `dismissId` |

### Above the header

`"above-header"` is the banner for something that applies to the whole
app and that people should see on every page. Put it after the `Header`
in the markup. It is fixed, so it still draws on top, and the skip link
in the header stays the first <DocKbd label="Tab" /> stop.

### Content

`"content"` is the default. Render the banner as the first child of
`Content`. Because it sits inside `Content`, it follows the side navigation's
width in every mode, including the rail, a resizable nav, a collapsed nav,
and right-to-left text. It bleeds across the content's 2rem padding so it
sits flush under the header, which assumes the default padding.

### Sticky

Set `sticky` to keep a `"content"` banner under the header while the page
scrolls. It also stays under an above-header banner.

<FileSource src="/framed/ShellBanner/ShellBannerContent" />

## Variants

### Kinds

Set `kind` to match the message. The status icon has a label, such as
"Warning", so screen readers convey the kind without color.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <ShellBanner kind="info" title="This is the staging environment." />
  <ShellBanner kind="warning" title="Billing is read-only on Saturday." />
  <ShellBanner kind="success" title="All systems are operational." />
  <ShellBanner kind="error" title="Some API requests are failing." />
</Stack>

### Thin

Set `thin` for a 4px strip in the kind's color, with the title in a tab
that hangs below it. It suits a quiet reminder, such as the environment
name. Give it a `title`, because the color alone does not say what the
strip means. A thin banner has no subtitle or actions.

The tab sits over the header's center, so keep that area of the header
free of controls.

<Stack gap={9}>
  <ShellBanner thin kind="warning" title="Staging" />
  <ShellBanner thin kind="info" title="Sandbox" dismissible />
</Stack>

### Low contrast

Banners are low contrast by default. Set `lowContrast` to `false` for the
high contrast variant, for a banner that has to stand out.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <ShellBanner lowContrast={false} kind="error" title="You are offline." />
  <ShellBanner lowContrast={false} kind="warning" title="Maintenance starts in 10 minutes." />
</Stack>

### Actions and subtitle

Add a `NotificationActionButton` in the "actions" slot. The banner's title
describes the button for screen readers, with no extra setup. Use `subtitle`
for a longer explanation.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <ShellBanner
    kind="warning-alt"
    title="Viewing as jane@acme.com."
    subtitle="This session is read only."
  >
    <svelte:fragment slot="actions">
      <NotificationActionButton>Exit</NotificationActionButton>
    </svelte:fragment>
  </ShellBanner>
</Stack>

## Dismissible

Set `dismissible` to show a close button. Closing dispatches a cancelable
`close` event, so a handler can keep the banner open with
`event.preventDefault()`. Bind `open` to close or reopen the banner from
your own code.

Give the banner a `dismissId` to remember the dismissal in `localStorage`,
under the key `ccs-shell-banner:{dismissId}`. It stays closed on later
visits and in other tabs until the id changes, so give each announcement
its own id.

<FileSource src="/framed/ShellBanner/ShellBannerDismissible" />

The server cannot read `localStorage`. A dismissed banner is in the server
markup, and closes once the page mounts, so it flashes for one frame. To
avoid that, read a cookie on the server and set `open` to `false`.

When the close button holds focus, closing moves focus back to the element
that had it before, or to `Content` when that takes focus.

## Announcements

A banner that is there when the page loads is part of the page, so it is a
labeled region, not a live region. Set `announce` for a banner that appears
while the page is open, such as going offline. It becomes `role="status"`,
and screen readers announce it politely. A banner never uses `role="alert"`.

Set `label` to name the region for screen readers. The default is
"Site announcement".

| Do | Why |
| :-- | :-- |
| Set `announce` only on a banner that appears after load | A banner at load would be read before the page, on every page |
| Give each banner a `label` that says what it is | Screen reader users list landmarks by name |
| Set a `title` on a `thin` banner | The strip's color is not a text alternative |
| Keep one `"above-header"` banner at a time | Each one writes the shell offset |
| Give a persistent announcement a `dismissId` | A new `dismissId` brings it back for the next announcement |

## One at a time

Two above-header banners would both write `--ccs-shell-offset`, and the
last one mounted wins. Keep a list of notices in priority order and show
the first one that applies.

<FileSource src="/framed/ShellBanner/ShellBannerPriority" />

## Keyboard

<DocKbd label="Tab" /> moves to the banner's actions, then its close button.
There is no arrow key navigation, and <DocKbd label="Escape" /> does nothing:
a banner is not a dialog.
